addenda={
 'screq_carter_1990':dict(status='supporting_page_checked',checked_on=DAY,note='Primary preface ix–x, title/imprint and contents now read in the publisher-distributor preview https://api.pageplace.de/preview/DT0400.9780773562431_A23655373/preview-9780773562431_A23655373.pdf. This is the reprint marked 2000, retaining copyright/legal deposit 1990 and first paperback 1993, not a newly published book in 2000. Carter explains that source research changed her initial cultural-aversion hypothesis and argues that many, not all, reserve residents sought farming while policy and later land surrender obstructed it. The rest of the monograph, including the agricultural-policy chapters, remains unread. This bounded primary reading supplements rather than erases the earlier metadata-only witness or its pre-paradigm chronology qualification.'),
 'screq_carter_2008':dict(status='supporting_page_checked',checked_on=DAY,note='Title/imprint and opening chapter pp. 3–9 now read in the publisher’s OA first-edition PDF, also archived by Library and Archives Canada: https://epe.lac-bac.gc.ca/100/200/300/athabasca_univ/importance_being/Sarah_Carter.pdf?nodisclaimer=1 . Imprint explicitly identifies University of Alberta Press AND AU Press, Athabasca University, first edition/first printing 2008. Title is “to 1915”; the current AU landing-page heading’s “in 1915” is not copied. The read opening connects marriage, gender and nation-building while stating that state power was limited/contested and Indigenous marriage/divorce law persisted. Remaining chapters and the later conclusion unread; no wholesale verification of the book or its quoted historical sources.'),
 'p4b_carter_2016':dict(status='supporting_page_checked',checked_on=DAY,note='Publisher OA copy now consulted through https://escholarship.umanitoba.ca/projects/imperial-plots : title/imprint plus introduction pp. 3–11 and 19–23 read. This copy explicitly says copyright 2016, “Reprinted with corrections 2018”; first-printing text not collated. P. 6 expressly cites Patrick Wolfe’s elimination argument; pp. 9–10 discuss reproduction and women’s uneven privileges/complicity, and pp. 19–20 describe transnational and imperial scales. This updates the earlier publisher-page-only observation: the book’s read introduction does engage settler-colonial arguments, although that phrase was absent from the publisher description. No general attribution of her earlier work to Wolfe follows. Rest of the book unread; source-specific claims are attributed to the corrected reprint actually consulted.')
}

